<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabernacle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mishkan, Tent of Meeting, Tent of the Congregation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the Tabernacle was the portable dwelling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> until the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. Exodus 25–31 and 35–40 describe it: a Holy of Holies containing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, behind a veil, and an outer sanctuary with a lamp-stand, a table for showbread and an altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>. Those who read the books of Kings literally hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple replaced it after 480 years in the Masoretic Text or 440 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>. Scholars compare it with tent shrines of Canaan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, among them a Midianite tent shrine at Timna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabernacle.jpg" alt="Tabernacle" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruk7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stiftshuette_Modell_Timnapark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> word mishkan means a residence or dwelling place, and the English word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> tabernaculum, meaning tent or hut. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> renders it with the Greek skene, itself a Semitic loanword for tent. The biblical scholar Michael B. Hundley argues that the Priestly source uses the terms tabernacle and tent of meeting together to stress the tent's double function as a divine dwelling on earth and a place where the god meets his people.</p><p>In Exodus the Tabernacle was a rectangular enclosure with a fence of fabric, poles and staked cords, set up whenever the tribes camped and oriented to the east. Inside stood a sanctuary draped with goat-hair curtains, roofed with rams' skins. Its inner room, the cube-shaped Holy of Holies, held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, which contained the two stone tablets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, a golden urn of manna and Aaron's rod. Bezalel, son of Uri, of the tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, was the main builder, assisted by Oholiab and skilled artisans. Priests burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> twice a day, and only they could enter the innermost room, once a year on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur">Yom Kippur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> the main camp was at Gilgal and the Tabernacle was moved to Shiloh in Ephraimite territory, where it stayed through most of the rule of the Judges. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> captured the Ark its history is separate from the Ark's. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">King Saul</a> it was moved to Nob and, after Saul massacred the priests there, to the hill-shrine at Gibeon. David placed the Ark in a tent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, while the Tabernacle remained at Gibeon, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> brought the structure and its furnishings to the Temple. The Tanakh does not mention it after the destruction of Jerusalem by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> in about 587 BCE.</p><p>Traditional scholars hold that the description reflects a real tabernacle, pointing to parallels with structures from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>. Mark S. Smith and William G. Dever, among others, derive it from tent shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and Egypt, such as the Midianite tent shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> in the Timna Valley.</p><p>Liane Feldman sees contrasting descriptions in the Pentateuch, a large ornate tent in the Priestly source and a smaller one for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> in another strand. Some scholars believe the Tabernacle as described may never have existed but reflects the cultic objects and imagery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Synagogues are said to follow its outlines, with an ark at the front for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> scrolls, a lamp that is always lit and a raised bimah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tabernacle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and Hymns for the Use of Jewish Children (1905), a closer one attributed to Frederick de Sola Mendes in The Jewish Encyclopedia (1906) and the Union Hymnal (1914), and two 21st-century versions that draw on these, in the Koren Sacks Siddur (2009) and the machzor <b>Mishkan</b> HaNefesh (2015).</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li></ul></section>
